"── 95_winui_focus.mst — WG4 D4: Do It / Print It, tracked by FOCUS ──────
 docs/ROADMAP.md's WG4 row ends `Do It/Print It enablement tracked by focus`,
 and §2.1 says the same thing from the other side: *greyed by focus context —
 the Mac contract, stated and tested*.

 WHAT WAS THERE BEFORE, and why it had to change. WG4's first pass tracked the
 ACTIVE VIEW: the verbs were enabled when `#workspace` was showing. That is a
 coarser thing that happens to agree today only because there is exactly one
 view that could ever supply source. It stops agreeing the moment WG5's
 Browser puts four panes in one view, or the moment a Workspace shares a
 window with a search field — and the failure would be silent: a Do It that
 evaluates the wrong pane's text.

 WHY POLL RATHER THAN LISTEN, which is the one real decision here. Win32 sends
 `WM_SETFOCUS`/`WM_KILLFOCUS` to the control that gains or loses focus — NOT
 to its parent. A shell that wanted them delivered would have to subclass every
 child's wndproc, which means a Rust trampoline per control class and a second
 door with none of the first one's guarantees. `GetFocus` answers the same
 question in one call, and the drain already runs on a 50 ms heartbeat, so
 asking there costs one Win32 call per pass and no new mechanism at all.

 The check is CHEAP AND IDEMPOTENT: it compares the focused HWND against the
 last one it saw and does nothing when they match, so the common case — focus
 did not move — is one `GetFocus` and a compare."

Object subclass: WinApi [
    WinApi class >> getFocus ^ <Integer> [
        "The window with the keyboard focus ON THIS THREAD, or 0. Thread-scoped
         by design, which is exactly right here: the UI VM owns the only thread
         that has any of these windows."
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetFocus ret: #g args: #()>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'GetFocus'
    ]

    WinApi class >> setFocus: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Give the keyboard focus to a control. Present for the same reason
         `GetFocus` is — the shell must be able to put focus where a view
         expects it on show (WG5's Workspace wants it on its text), and a
         gate must be able to MOVE focus to prove the predicate answers both
         ways rather than merely answering false consistently."
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetFocus ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'SetFocus'
    ]

    WinApi class >> getWindowLongPtr: hwnd <Integer> index: n <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetWindowLongPtrW ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'GetWindowLongPtrW'
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinControl [
    "── can this control supply source to Do It? ─────────────────────────
     A control is a SOURCE SURFACE when it is an editable multiline EDIT: the
     Workspace is one, a read-only view placeholder is not, a button is not,
     and the transcript — read-only by construction — is not either.

     Asked of WIN32 rather than remembered, so a control whose style is changed
     by anything at all still answers correctly. That is the same rule
     `barLabel` follows and for the same reason."

    isSourceSurface ^ <Boolean> [
        | style |
        (klass = 'EDIT') ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        style := WinApi getWindowLongPtr: handle index: (0 - 16).   "GWL_STYLE"
        ^((style bitAnd: (WinApi constant: 'ES_MULTILINE')) ~= 0)
            and: [ (style bitAnd: (WinApi constant: 'ES_READONLY')) = 0 ]
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [

    WinShell class >> focusedControl ^ <Object> [
        "The WinControl that owns the keyboard focus, or nil — including nil
         when focus is on the shell window itself or on nothing."
        | h |
        h := WinApi getFocus.
        h = 0 ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^self controls detect: [ :c | c handle = h ] ifNone: [ nil ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> focusedControlName ^ <Object> [
        ^self focusedControl ifNil: [ nil ] ifNotNil: [ :c | c name ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> syncCommandEnablement [
        "D4's predicate, and it is deliberately about the FOCUSED CONTROL
         rather than the active view: `can the thing the user is typing in
         supply source?`. Answers false for a view with nothing editable in
         it, which is the honest answer, and lights up on its own for the
         Workspace and the Browser's source pane.

         BUT A BAR CELL IS NOT AN ANSWER TO THAT QUESTION, and missing that
         made the verbs unusable in the most literal way. Clicking `Do It`
         gives the BUTTON focus; the button is not a source surface; so this
         method disabled it — and a disabled button never sends WM_COMMAND.
         The verb switched itself off on the way down and the click vanished.
         Reported as `no response from print it or doit`, and correctly.

         It survived D4's own tests because those set focus synthetically and
         asserted both directions — editable EDIT true, read-only false — and
         never made the ONE transition that happens every time a human uses
         the thing: source surface → verb cell. A test can prove a rule holds
         and still miss that the rule defeats itself.

         So a bar cell is TRANSPARENT here: focus moving onto one leaves the
         enablement exactly as the last real surface left it. That is also
         what the user means — they have not stopped pointing at their code by
         reaching for the button that acts on it."
        | focused on |
        focused := self focusedControl.
        (focused notNil and: [ self isBarControl: focused name ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        on := focused
            ifNil: [ false ]
            ifNotNil: [ :c | c isSourceSurface ].
        "IDEMPOTENT, and that is what makes it safe to call every pass. A pass
         where nothing moved computes one Boolean, compares it, and touches no
         control at all — so reconciling constantly costs the same as the old
         change-detection did, without depending on the detection being right."
        on = CommandsEnabled ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        CommandsEnabled := on.
        #(#doit #printit #accept) do: [ :n |
            (self controlNamed: n) ifNotNil: [ :c |
                c enable: on.
                "Enablement is part of an owner-drawn cell's PICTURE — the
                 label greys — so a cell whose state just changed is stale by
                 definition. Invalidating here rather than at the call site
                 means no caller can forget."
                WinApi invalidateRect: c handle rect: 0 erase: 0 ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> checkFocusChanged ^ <Boolean> [
        "Called from the drain. Answers whether focus MOVED — which callers use
         to know a real transition happened; enablement itself no longer
         depends on that answer (see below).

         `GetFocus` is one call and the compare is one word; a pass in which
         nothing moved costs exactly that and touches no control."
        | h moved |
        h := WinApi getFocus.
        moved := h ~= LastFocusHwnd.
        LastFocusHwnd := h.
        moved ifTrue: [
            FocusChanges := (FocusChanges isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ FocusChanges ]) + 1 ].
        "RECONCILE ON EVERY PASS, not only when the handle moved. The old code
         made enablement a consequence of DETECTING a change, and the detection
         is a cached handle — so any way of getting out of step stuck the verbs
         in whatever state they were last left in, permanently.

         That is not hypothetical: measured on a freshly opened window whose
         Workspace held focus and answered `isSourceSurface` true, with the
         verbs disabled and `focusChanges` frozen at 1. Nothing recovered it,
         because nothing ever asked again.

         `syncCommandEnablement` is idempotent and early-outs when the answer
         is unchanged, so a quiet pass costs one GetFocus, one lookup and one
         Boolean compare — what the compare above already cost. The state is
         now self-healing rather than event-sourced, which is the right shape
         for a fact that can be re-derived cheaply at any time."
        self syncCommandEnablement.
        "The verb cells' repaint now belongs to `syncCommandEnablement`, which
         is the only thing that can change how they look and so the only thing
         that should have to remember. Duplicating it here would repaint three
         cells on every focus move that changed nothing about them — including
         every click on the bar itself."
        ^moved
    ]

    WinShell class >> focusChanges ^ <Integer> [
        ^FocusChanges isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ FocusChanges ] ]
]
